1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,723
Before, you'd waste time

2
00:00:01,723 --> 00:00:04,569
after every Google Meet call writing up the plan;

3
00:00:04,569 --> 00:00:06,789
now Claude Meetings writes it with you

4
00:00:06,789 --> 00:00:08,440
before you hang up, but how?

5
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:10,681
Here's how, in one planning call.

6
00:00:11,181 --> 00:00:12,806
Your team plans an event.

7
00:00:12,806 --> 00:00:15,273
"What did last year's cost?" "I'll check later."

8
00:00:15,273 --> 00:00:17,186
You hang up, and someone still writes

9
00:00:17,186 --> 00:00:18,394
the notes and the plan.

10
00:00:18,894 --> 00:00:20,635
Same call with Claude Meetings,

11
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:23,330
rolling out in beta since November 12.

12
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:25,838
Claude joins as a visible participant.

13
00:00:25,838 --> 00:00:27,766
"Claude, start a doc for this plan."

14
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:30,933
It writes up what the team said, while teammates edit.

15
00:00:30,933 --> 00:00:32,251
Last year's cost?

16
00:00:32,251 --> 00:00:33,959
Claude asks first, then looks it up

17
00:00:33,959 --> 00:00:35,492
and posts it in the chat.

18
00:00:35,492 --> 00:00:37,161
The timeline goes on a whiteboard.

19
00:00:37,661 --> 00:00:39,950
Claude writes the notes and action items,

20
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:41,885
so no one on the call has to.

21
00:00:41,885 --> 00:00:44,219
After the call, it's all on the meeting's page.

22
00:00:44,719 --> 00:00:47,084
That's how the plan is written before you hang up:

23
00:00:47,084 --> 00:00:48,683
it's built during the call.

24
00:00:48,683 --> 00:00:51,592
The catch: it's a beta, for Team and Enterprise,

25
00:00:51,592 --> 00:00:53,314
on Google Meet only for now.

26
00:00:53,814 --> 00:00:56,246
Same call; the writing happens while you talk.

27
00:00:56,746 --> 00:00:58,148
Subscribe for the next one.
